WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.329
Welcome to week six.

2
00:00:01.379 --> 00:00:07.883
The theme: a service has an owner, a cost per task and an SLA, a service-level agreement.

3
00:00:07.933 --> 00:00:15.816
This video is about cost, because it is the number an executive will ask for first, and the one most teams cannot produce.

4
00:00:16.566 --> 00:00:22.401
Model providers charge per token, separately for input and output, usually priced per million tokens.

5
00:00:22.451 --> 00:00:25.932
Output tokens cost several times more than input tokens.

6
00:00:25.982 --> 00:00:31.818
Input that the provider has seen recently and cached costs a fraction of fresh input.

7
00:00:31.868 --> 00:00:37.310
And some features, such as very long contexts or built-in tools, carry extra charges.

8
00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:40.057
The exact numbers change every few months.

9
00:00:40.107 --> 00:00:41.626
The structure does not.

10
00:00:41.676 --> 00:00:43.980
For an agent, two things follow.

11
00:00:44.030 --> 00:00:49.866
Input is most of the bill, because the whole context is sent on every turn.

12
00:00:49.916 --> 00:00:58.105
And caching the stable part of the context, the instructions and the tool schemas, is the single cheapest optimization there is.

13
00:00:58.855 --> 00:01:01.568
Here is how a task's cost builds up.

14
00:01:01.618 --> 00:01:06.404
Each turn sends the whole context so far: instructions, tools, history, the latest result.

15
00:01:06.454 --> 00:01:10.894
So the input for turn six includes everything from turns one to five.

16
00:01:10.944 --> 00:01:14.003
The output of each turn is small by comparison.

17
00:01:14.053 --> 00:01:18.148
Add up the turns and you have the cost of one task.

18
00:01:18.198 --> 00:01:25.746
Multiply by the number of runs, including the retries and the runs that failed, and you have the cost of the service.

19
00:01:25.796 --> 00:01:34.036
A task that takes twelve turns costs far more than twice a task that takes six, because the later turns carry the heavier context.

20
00:01:34.786 --> 00:01:40.182
You measure all of this from traces, which is why tracing was in the first lab.

21
00:01:40.232 --> 00:01:49.031
For each run: input, output and cached tokens per turn, which model served each call, wall-clock time, and how much of it was waiting on tools.

22
00:01:49.081 --> 00:01:56.519
For the service: cost per task, as a mean and as a 95th percentile, because the long tail is where budgets die.

23
00:01:56.569 --> 00:02:03.326
Cost per successful task, which is total cost divided by the number of passes, and which is the honest number.

24
00:02:03.376 --> 00:02:10.473
And the cost of a failure, which is what a person spends afterwards finding and fixing what the agent got wrong.

25
00:02:10.523 --> 00:02:15.578
That last one is usually the largest, and it never appears on the provider's invoice.

26
00:02:16.328 --> 00:02:18.159
Five ways to cut it.

27
00:02:18.209 --> 00:02:19.663
Cache the stable prefix.

28
00:02:19.713 --> 00:02:30.193
Route: use a small, cheap model for routine turns, classification and formatting, and a large model only where judgment is needed, and measure that the pass rate holds.

29
00:02:30.243 --> 00:02:34.330
Trim tool results and context, which you did in week three.

30
00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:42.228
Cap the number of steps, because a loop that does not stop is the most expensive failure an agent can have.

31
00:02:42.278 --> 00:02:48.621
And count the retries: when error analysis removes a failure category, cost per successful task falls too.

32
00:02:48.671 --> 00:02:51.253
Evaluation and economics are the same work.

33
00:02:52.003 --> 00:03:11.963
On your operations sheet, report cost per task and per successful task with the 95th percentile, latency as median and 95th percentile, the budget cap per task and what the harness does when it is hit, and the price of a human minute, so that the review and approval time from last week shows up as a number.

34
00:03:12.013 --> 00:03:17.137
Next video: watching the service in production, and knowing when a change made it worse.
